.TH MESG 1 
.SH NAME
mesg  \-  permit or deny messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mesg
[
.B n
] [
.B y
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mesg
with argument
.B n
forbids messages via
.IR  write (1)
by revoking non-user
write permission on the user's terminal.
.I Mesg
with argument
.B y
reinstates permission.
All by itself,
.I mesg
reports the current state without changing it.
The report goes to the standard error, which is also the descriptor
.I mesg
asks
.IR ttyname (3)
about.
.PP
.B Note:
the two modes are 0622 and 0600, and the terminal nodes of this system
are created 0622, so a fresh login receives messages.
.PP
.B Note:
.IR chmod (2)
here is gated on ownership, so
.I mesg
works only on a terminal the caller owns \-
which is arranged by
.IR login (1),
whose
.IR chown (2)
runs before it drops privilege.
.I Mesg
is deliberately not set-user-id: it would otherwise let any user
silence any terminal on the machine.
.SH FILES
/dev/tty*
.br
/dev
.SH "SEE ALSO"
write(1), wall(1M), who(1), chmod(2), ttyname(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if messages are receivable,
1 if not, 2 on error.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's program exited \-1 rather than 2 on an error, and this page
was already right about it: an exit status above 127 does not survive
.IR wait (2)
on this machine, so \-1 reached the shell as 255.
It is 2 now.
.PP
.B Note:
a process with no terminal \- a
.I mesg
in a shell script with its standard error redirected \-
now reports
.RB ` "mesg: not a tty" '
and exits 2, where v7 passed a null pointer to
.IR stat (2).
